this morning we're learning more about  two men accused of pretending to be  federal agents and giving financial  favors to secret service officials  including a member of first lady jill  biden's protective detail prosecutors  say this alleged scheme lasted for more  than two years but they haven't revealed  a motive senior investigative  correspondent catherine herridge is  following this story catherine good  morning well good morning federal  prosecutors told the court the men are a  flight risk citing possible links to  pakistani intelligence and recent travel  to iran and the judge agreed to hold  them pending a detention hearing later  today these images show the suspects  ariane taharzadeh and haider ali  surrounded then arrested by federal law  enforcement on wednesday two law  enforcement sources tell cbs news ali  had been under investigation in new  jersey by homeland security and the u.s  postal service concerning an alleged  credit card ring yesterday prosecutors  revealed evidence seized from the  suspect's luxury d.c apartments  including ballistic vests gas masks  handheld radios and a drone court  documents indicate tahrzadeh had gained  access to the security system for the  entire apartment complex which houses  many residents affiliated with federal  law enforcement agencies to win over  agents the suspect's alleged gifts  included rent-free apartments at a cost  of at least forty thousand dollars a  year iphones and a flat screen tv one  suspect offered to buy a two thousand  dollar assault rifle for a secret  service agent assigned to the first  lady's protective detail the secret  service says all personnel involved are  on administrative leave a former fbi  agent tells cbs news it appears to be a  significant security lapse by a handful  of agents and the amount of money  involved suggests the suspects had  outside support tony  all right catherine thank you very much
 

